{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793390324,"nonce":"64P9mqDNrpFbaF6XROtytn7nvRY=","title":"Canvas blend modes that read the target, nested masks, a live surface brush","message":"Left over from the OpenFL Graphics parity work (git-bug fb2e74ea3ab4caa6ee9aa0410cc60090e49b9fb7f1f092f3922db380d6e0279d).\n\n**Blend modes that read the target**\nOverlay, hardlight, difference, invert, darken, lighten, layer, alpha and erase cannot be a fixed GPU blend over a premultiplied source. They need the target's colour in the shader: suspend the pass, copy the region the run covers, and sample the copy. Erase also wants the canvas drawn into a layer of its own, so it clears the canvas's drawing and not the page behind it.\n\n**Masks that nest**\npushMask inside a mask is ignored. Coverage of two masks needs two mask textures sampled together, or the product drawn into one.\n\n**A live surface as a brush**\nPixelSurface.bitmap() encodes and decodes the whole surface after each write. Sampling the surface's own texture from PathShader, with its dirty-rectangle uploads, would let an updated bitmap fill or textured triangles cost what ctx.surface costs.\n\n**Edges of textured triangles**\ndrawTriangles and drawQuads are not antialiased and map the texture affinely; a rotated sprite is jagged and uvt's perspective term is not read.","files":null}]}